"[2601.09805] Improving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoning via Attention-Aware Intervention (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09805)\nciteturn30956view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09805\",\"lineno\":38}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Jan 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 24 Jan 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Improving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoning via Attention-Aware Intervention\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Nguyen Minh Phuong , cite7†Dang Huu Tien , cite8†Naoya Inoue L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Improving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoning via Attention-Aware Intervention, by Nguyen Minh Phuong and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Modern logical reasoning with LLMs primarily relies on employing complex interactive frameworks that decompose the reasoning process into subtasks solved through carefully designed prompts or requiring external resources (e.g., symbolic solvers) to exploit their strong logical structures. While interactive approaches introduce additional overhead or depend on external components, which limit their scalability.\nL17: In this work, we introduce a non-interactive, end-to-end framework for reasoning tasks, enabling reasoning to emerge within the model itself-improving generalization while preserving analyzability without any external resources. We show that introducing structural information into the few-shot prompt activates a subset of attention heads that patterns aligned with logical reasoning operators.\nL18: Building on this insight, we propose Attention-Aware Intervention (AAI), an inference-time intervention method that reweights attention scores across selected heads identified by their logical patterns. AAI offers an efficient way to steer the model's reasoning toward leveraging prior knowledge through attention modulation.\nL19: Extensive experiments show that AAI enhances logical reasoning performance across diverse benchmarks, and model architectures, while incurring negligible additional computational overhead. Code is available at cite11†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Comments:  | Findings of EACL 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2601.09805 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2601.09805v2 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.09805†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Phuong Nguyen Minh [cite15†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 14 Jan 2026 19:10:10 UTC (3,960 KB)\nL29: [v2] Sat, 24 Jan 2026 08:52:37 UTC (4,207 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Improving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoning via Attention-Aware Intervention, by Nguyen Minh Phuong and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite9†View PDF L38:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite16†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L47: \nL48: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-01 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite23†cs L53: cite24†cs.LG L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: