†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Jan 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 30 Aug 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Seoyeon Kim , cite7†Jaehyung Kim L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation, by Seoyeon Kim and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Personalizing Large Language Models typically relies on static retrieval or one-time adaptation, assuming user preferences remain invariant over time. However, real-world interactions are dynamic, where user interests continuously evolve, posing a challenge for models to adapt to preference drift without catastrophic forgetting.\nL17: Standard continual learning approaches often struggle in this context, as they indiscriminately update on noisy interaction streams, failing to distinguish genuine preference shifts from transient contexts. To address this, we introduce SPRInG, a novel semi-parametric framework designed for effective continual personalization.\nL18: During training, SPRInG employs drift-driven selective adaptation, which utilizes a likelihood-based scoring function to identify high-novelty interactions, selectively updating the user-specific adapter on drift signals while preserving hard-to-learn residuals in a replay buffer. During inference, we apply strict relevance gating and fuse parametric knowledge with retrieved history via probability interpolation.\nL19: Experiments on the long-form personalized generation benchmark demonstrate that SPRInG significantly outperforms existing baselines, validating its robustness for real-world continual personalization.\nL20: Comments:  | EMNLP 26 Main, 30 pages\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.7\nL23: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2601.09974 [cs.AI]\nL24:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2601.09974v3 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL25:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.09974†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Seoyeon Kim [cite13†view email ]\nL29: cite5†[v1] Thu, 15 Jan 2026 01:32:27 UTC (903 KB)\nL30: cite14†[v2] Mon, 10 Aug 2026 08:24:26 UTC (909 KB)\nL31: [v3] Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:23:21 UTC (913 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation, by Seoyeon Kim and 1 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite8†View PDF L39:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite15†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L43: \nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.AI\nL47: \nL48: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L49: \nL50: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-01 L51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: cite22†cs L55: cite23†cs.CL L56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL84: \nL85: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL86: \nL87: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL88: \nL89: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL90: \nL91: scite Smart Citations (cite30†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL92: \nL93: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL94: # Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\nL95: \nL96: [Input] alphaXiv Toggle\nL97: \nL98: alphaXiv (cite31†What is alphaXiv?†alphaxiv.org )\nL99: \nL100: [Input] Links to Code Toggle\nL101: \nL102: CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (cite32†What is CatalyzeX?†www.catalyzex.com )\nL103: \nL104: [Input] DagsHub Toggle\nL105: \nL106: DagsHub (cite33†What is DagsHub?†dagshub.com )\nL107: \nL108: [Input] GotitPub Toggle\nL109: \nL110: Gotit.pub (cite34†What is GotitPub?†gotit.pub )\nL111: \nL112: [Input] Huggingface Toggle\nL113: \nL114: Hugging Face (cite35†What is Huggingface?†huggingface.co )\nL115: \nL116: [Input] ScienceCast Toggle\nL117: ScienceCast (cite36†What is ScienceCast?†sciencecast.org )\nL118: \nL119: [Input] Demos\nL120: # Demos\nL121: \nL122: [Input] Replicate Toggle\nL123: \nL124: Replicate (cite37†What is Replicate?†replicate.com )\nL125: \nL126: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL127: \nL128: Hugging Face Spaces (cite38†What is Spaces?†huggingface.co )\nL129: \nL130: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL131: \nL132: TXYZ.AI (cite39†What is TXYZ.AI?†txyz.ai )\nL133: \nL134: [Input] Related Papers\nL135: # Recommenders and Search Tools\nL136: \nL137: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL138: \nL139: Influence Flower (cite40†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL140: \nL141: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL142: \nL143: CORE Recommender (cite41†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL144: \nL145:   * Author\nL146:   * Venue\nL147:   * Institution\nL148:   * Topic\nL149: \nL150: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL151: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL152: \nL153: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL154: \nL155: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL156: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite42†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL157: \nL158: cite43†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite44†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL159: \nL160: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite45†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL161: cite46†About†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L162: \nL163: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30956view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"lineno\":536}); Total lines: 703\nL504: This diagonal distribution indicates that high-quality samples which are linguistically well-formed tend to naturally align with high-novelty signals. The relatively vacant top-left quadrant (low quality, high novelty) suggests that our benchmark datasets contain minimal pseudo-novelty from gibberish or noise.\nL505: This qualitative analysis confirms that instances involving irrelevant system metadata or extremely short text lengths are relegated to the bottom-left quadrant, as they lack both linguistic coherence and informative value for learning (Table  cite103†7 ).\nL506: #### Robustness to balancing parameter $\\alpha$.\nL507: The structural coupling between $Q$ and $N$ in the high-fidelity region explains the empirical robustness of our method to the choice of the balancing weight $\\alpha$. As shown in Table cite104†6 , varying $\\alpha$ from 0.0 to $\\infty$ yields nearly identical performance in both ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-L scores across tasks.\nL508: Since high-quality and high-novelty samples largely overlap in the top-right region of the distribution, the same group of informative samples is consistently chosen as drift samples regardless of the balancing weight $\\alpha$. Nevertheless, we maintain $\\alpha=1.0$ as a precautionary safeguard in practical deployments to explicitly penalize potental noise and ensure that only linguistically coherent signals are selected for adaptation.\nL509: #### Distinguishing drift score from hard example mining.\nL510: \nL511: A potential concern is whether our drift score simply performs conventional hard example mining by targeting samples with low $p_{\\texttt{adap}}$. To clarify the distinction, we define the Hardness Term ($H$) of a sample as the negative log-likelihood under the adapted model.\nL512: \nL513:  | $$H=-\\log p_{\\texttt{adap}}$$  |  | (10)\nL514: \nL515: Using Eq. cite105†9 , the hardness term can be written as\nL516: \nL517:  | $$H=-\\log p_{\\texttt{adap}}=N-\\log p_{\\texttt{base}}.$$  |  | (11)\nL518: Since $Q=\\alpha\\log p_{\\texttt{base}}$, for $\\alpha\\neq 0$ this is equivalently\nL519: \nL520:  | $$H=N-\\frac{Q}{\\alpha}.$$  |  | (12)\nL521: \nL522: We obtain $H=N-Q$ when $\\alpha=1.0$.\nL523: Eq. cite106†12 shows that $H$ aggregates two different factors: (i) the lack of model adaptation captured by the Novelty Term ($N$), and (ii) the lack of linguistic fluency or coherence reflected by the Quality Term ($-Q$) (with $\\alpha=1.0$). As a result, a high hardness score can arise either from true under-adaptation (large $N$) or from intrinsically low-quality and ambiguous instances (low $Q$), which are not necessarily indicative of preference drift.\nL524: Therefore, using $H$ alone tends to over-select globally difficult or noisy samples that are hard for both the base and adapted models, making it an inconsistent and potentially misleading proxy for identifying genuine preference drift.\nL525: cite107†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Correlation between hardness and novelty terms. The scatter plots show the relationship between the Hardness Term and the Novelty Term. Spearman’s $\\rho$ is $-0.2361$ for Abstract Generation and $0.3468$ for Review Writing. cite108†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: Correlation between hardness term and drift score. The scatter plots show the relationship between the Hardness Term and the Drift Score.\nL526: Spearman’s $\\rho$ is $-0.6997$ for Abstract Generation and $-0.3421$ for Review Writing.\nL527: As shown in Figure cite109†5 , the correlation between $H$ and $N$ is not consistent across tasks ($\\rho=-0.2361$ for Abstract Generation and $\\rho=0.3468$ for Review Writing), indicating that hardness is not an equivalent measure for novelty. Moreover, Figure cite110†6 shows that $H$ and the Drift Score $D$ are negatively correlated in both tasks ($\\rho=-0.6997$ and $\\rho=-0.3421$). This implies that many high-hardness instances are also difficult for the base model, which lowers their drift scores.\nL528: Together, these results support our claim that the proposed drift score is not conventional hard example mining. Instead, it prioritizes well-formed and plausible instances where the adapted model cannot cover well, rather than globally hard or noisy samples.\nL529: #### Visualization of selected drift samples.\nL530: \nL531: cite111†Image: Refer to caption Figure 7: Visualization of sample selections by Hardness Term versus Drift Score. Gray points denote all samples, while red and blue highlight the top 30% samples by the Hardness Term and the Drift Score, respectively.\nL532: Figure cite112†7 contrasts the samples selected by top-30% hardness and top-30% drift score on the quality–novelty plane. The hardness criterion (red) highlights a broad diagonal band and extends into lower-quality areas, indicating that many hard instances are globally difficult and may also be poorly explained by the base model. In contrast, our drift criterion (blue) concentrates on the upper-right wedge where both quality and novelty are high.\nL533: This visualization supports that our drift score performs mismatch-driven selection and filters out low-quality and noisy hard cases that are unlikely to provide reliable personalization signals.\nL534: ### D.2 Inference dynamics: relevance gating\nL535: \nL536: Figure 8: Distribution of retrieved interactions. We compare the retrieval coverage under three threshold settings: no threshold ($\\sigma=0$), Q2, and Q3 (ours). Table 8: Effectiveness of relevance gating. Performance comparison using thresholds based on BM25 score quartiles.\nL537: Methods Threshold $\\sigma$  | Abstract Generation  | Review Writing\nL538: --- | --- | ---\nL539: ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL540: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL541: 0  | 0.351  | 0.191  | 0.319  | 0.143\nL542: Q2  | 0.350  | 0.190  | 0.323  | 0.142\nL543: Q3 (Ours)  | 0.350  | 0.190  | 0.326  | 0.142\nL544: We investigate the impact of our relevance gating strategy (Table cite113†8 ), which filters retrieved contexts based on task-specific thresholds. Combined with the distribution analysis in Figure cite114†8 , we observe that SPRInG exhibits adaptive behavior tailored to the relevance characteristics of each task. In Abstract Generation, where interactions share strong semantic continuity, the strict Q3 threshold still permits context for over 92% of queries.\nL545: This indicates that most retrieved information is highly relevant, allowing the model to maintain robust performance while filtering only the most extreme outliers. Conversely, in Review Writing, which is characterized by more diverse text content, indiscriminately incorporating retrieved history can be detrimental. The Q3 threshold aggressively filters out 58.6% of queries that fall below the relevance cutoff. Despite this harsh filtering, the model achieves a 2.19% relative gain in ROUGE-1.\nL546: This demonstrates that our relevance gating effectively identifies and excludes interfering information, ensuring that the model only attends to truly relevant context.\nL547: ## Appendix E Qualitative Analysis\nL548: ### E.1 Qualitative comparison with baselines\nL549: Table 9: Qualitative comparison of Abstract Generation.\nL550: Query  | Generate an abstract for the title “An effective teaching-learning-based optimization algorithm for the flexible job-shop scheduling problem with fuzzy processing time.”\nL551: --- | ---\nL552: User Answer  | In this paper, an effective teaching–learning-based optimization algorithm (TLBO) is proposed to solve the flexible job-shop problem with fuzzy processing time (FJSPF). First, a special encoding scheme is used to represent solutions, and a decoding method is employed to transfer a solution to a feasible schedule in the fuzzy sense.\nL553: Second, a bi-phase crossover scheme based on the teaching–learning mechanism and special local search operators are incorporated into the search framework of the TLBO to balance the exploration and exploitation capabilities. Moreover, the influence of the key parameters on the TLBO is investigated using the Taguchi method. Finally, numerical results based on some benchmark instances and the comparisons with some existing algorithms are provided.\nL554: The comparative results demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of the proposed TLBO algorithm in solving the FJSPF.\nL555: Base  | **Keywords:** Flexible Job Shop Scheduling, Optimization Algorithm, Teaching Learning Paradigm, Fuzzy Logic, Multi-Objective.**This research investigates a complex operational challenge – optimizing schedules within a flexible job shop environment where tasks possess inherently uncertain durations represented by fuzzy logic. The proposed approach leverages a novel teaching learning paradigm to develop an efficient and adaptive scheduling solution.\nL556: Departing from traditional deterministic methods, this work introduces an iterative process wherein a ẗeacherägent guides a student scheduler through exploration of the vast search space, progressively refining its decision-making capabilities based on observed performance feedback. The core innovation lies in dynamically adjusting the teacher’s guidance strategy shifting between prescriptive rule application and exploratory heuristic generation—to balance convergence speed and optimality.\nL557: Furthermore, we address the inherent multi-objective nature of job shop scheduling (e.g., minimizing makespan while maximizing resource utilization) by incorporating these objectives into both the student’s objective function and the teacher’s reward system. This results in a robust and adaptable algorithm capable of generating high-quality solutions even under conditions of significant uncertainty and complexity.\nL558: Ultimately, the presented method offers a promising avenue toward intelligent scheduling systems that can autonomously learn optimal strategies across diverse and evolving manufacturing landscapes.**\nL559: RAG each  | In this paper, we propose an effective Teaching-Learning-Based Optimization (TLBO) algorithm tailored for addressing the complex challenges presented by the flexible job-shop scheduling problem incorporating fuzzy processing times.  The core innovation lies in adapting the traditional TLBO framework to handle uncertainty inherent within the processing durations. We utilize a novel representation scheme leveraging fuzzy sets to capture the probabilistic nature of task execution times.\nL560: Simultaneously, a refined learning process dynamically adjusts the evolutionary dynamics, promoting efficient convergence while mitigating premature convergence issues often encountered when dealing with imprecise data. Further enhancing performance, a sensitivity analysis using the Taguchi methodology identifies optimal parameter configurations for robust adaptation across varying levels of fuzziness.\nL561: Experimental validation against established benchmarks demonstrates the superiority of our approach compared to conventional methods, showcasing its efficacy in optimizing schedules under conditions of uncertain processing times within the context of the flexible job-shop environment.\nL562: CAMA.  | A novel hybrid approach is proposed to tackle a complex, real-world manufacturing challenge—the Flexible Job Shop Scheduling (FJSS) Problem. The FJSS involves multiple machines and operations that can be performed in different orders depending on various factors such as machine availability or operator skill levels. This paper presents a new method combining genetic algorithms (GAs) and simulated annealing (SAs), which has been named GA/SA.\nL563: In this study, we propose a new encoding scheme based on the permutation representation of jobs and operators. We also introduce two new crossover and mutation operators designed specifically for the GA/SA framework. Finally, we test our GA/SA model using several benchmark instances from the literature and compare its performance against other state-of-art methods. Results show that GA/SA outperforms all existing approaches by achieving better solutions within reasonable computation times\nL564: SPRInG (Ours)  | This paper presents a novel Teaching-Learning-Based Optimization (TLBO) algorithm for tackling the flexible job-shop scheduling problem (FJSP) with fuzzy processing times. FJSPs are notoriously complex due to the inherent uncertainty in processing times and the need to accommodate flexible job routings.\nL565: Our TLBO algorithm incorporates adaptive learning rates and a novel penalty function to effectively handle the fuzzy nature of processing times and the multi-objective nature of the problem. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm through extensive simulations on benchmark FJSP instances, comparing it against several state-of-the-art scheduling algorithms.\nL566: Results show that the proposed TLBO algorithm provides a competitive solution in terms of makespan and job completion time, while maintaining a good level of solution diversity.\nL567:  |\nL568: Table 10: Qualitative comparison of Review Writing.\nL569: Query  | Generate a detailed review for a product ’Brooklyn Bean Roastery Bold Variety Pack Single-Cup Coffee for Keurig K-Cup Brewers, 40 Count’ with the following description: ’In Brooklyn, we know a thing or two about being bold and when we say this coffee is bold you betta believe it’s bold. To get a perfect dark roast, the beans are removed from the roasters right before they get to their peak roasting point.\nL570: This helps them retain their sharp floral notes and all the smoky goodness without any of the burnt aftertaste. When these beans with big-time flavor are brewed through our patented tension- based filters, they create a Cup of Joe that will delight lovers of good strong coffee. The Bold Variety Pack includes: Boardwalk Blend, Corner Donut Shop, Cyclone, Express-O, French Roast and Fuhgeddaboutit.’. The review has an overall rating of ’5.0’. The review summary is: ’Medium roast, bright and fruity’.\nL571: --- | ---\nL572: User Answer  | This is a medium roast (not burnt, like French Roast) and clearly dominated by Central American coffees–it’s acidic and bright but the mid notes are smooth and there is a citrus-fruitiness. 